      "style": "Haunting Ambient Folk / Cello Requiem",
      "theory": "The destruction of the entry-level career rung: generative AI and automated agents eliminate apprentice and junior roles (junior developers, paralegals, copywriters, analysts). Without entry points into cognitive professions, generational social mobility collapses entirely, creating an impassable barrier for the young.",
      "summary": "A haunting, atmospheric track that confronts the end of social mobility. The protagonist observes his children studying for careers that technically no longer exist. He realizes that Generative AI hasn't just replaced experts; it has destroyed the \"training ground\" where expertise is formed1. The \"bottom rungs\" of the corporate ladder—where he started his climb—have been sawed off by automation2. The music is Dark Ambient / Glitch Folk. The Cello is heavily processed, sounding like it is \"drowning\" under layers of digital noise, representing the suffocation of human potential.",
      "cleanLyrics": "[Verse 1]\nI watch my daughter studying the law\nShe is reading cases with a sense of awe\nShe wants to be a paralegal first\nTo quench her intellectual thirst\nBut I have seen the software in the cloud\nIt reads a million pages in a shroud\nIt writes the brief before she finds her pen\nThere is no room for junior women or men\n\n[Chorus]\nThe Algorithm ate the ladder today\nThe bottom rungs have all been cut away\nThere is no place to stand and learn the trade\nThe entry level path has been unmade\nYou cannot climb if you cannot start\nThe machine has taken the beating heart\nThe Algorithm ate the ladder today\n\n[Verse 2]\nMy son is learning Python and C Plus\nHe thinks the code is something he can trust\nHe wants to be a junior engineer\nBut the junior role just disappeared last year\nThe AI writes the boilerplate for free\nIt doesn't need a salary or a fee\nHow do you become a master of the art\nWhen they automate the very first part?\n\n[Bridge]\nThey pulled the bridge up to the castle wall\nAnd left the rest of us outside to fall\nIt’s not a ceiling made of glass\nIt’s a broken floor for the working class\nThe stepping stones are turned to sand\nBy the invisible digital hand\n\n[Chorus]\nThe Algorithm ate the ladder today\nThe bottom rungs have all been cut away\nThere is no place to stand and learn the trade\nThe entry level path has been unmade\nYou cannot climb if you cannot start\nThe machine has taken the beating heart\nThe Algorithm ate the ladder today\n\n[Outro]\nDon't look up, kids.\nThere is nothing there."
